    a: "Companies borrow in two main markets: loans, arranged by banks and sold to institutional investors or made directly by private credit funds, and bonds, sold to investors under an indenture. Ratings split borrowers into investment grade and high yield; leveraged finance serves the second group, often funding buyouts, acquisitions and refinancings. Debt is priced as a spread over a benchmark: SOFR for floating-rate loans, Treasuries for fixed-rate bonds. Lenders protect themselves with collateral, priority, covenants and call protection, and judge a credit by how reliably cash flow covers the debt.",
