    type: "qa",
    q: "What do rating agencies look at when they rate a company, and why can two companies with the same leverage get different ratings?",
    a: "They combine business risk (how reliable the earnings are: cyclicality, market position, scale, diversification and margins) with financial risk (how heavy the debt is: leverage, cash flow to debt, coverage and liquidity), then adjust for financial policy and governance. Steadier earnings can carry more debt, so the same 3.5x leverage can be BBB for one company and BB− for another.",
    why: "Leverage alone misleads because EBITDA's reliability varies: a utility's is steady, a chipmaker's can halve. Agencies anchor on business risk, then read the financial ratios against it, using their own adjusted figures: they add leases and pension deficits to debt, give little credit for adjusted-EBITDA add-backs and look forward through the cycle. Financial policy matters too. Sponsor-owned companies are often rated lower than their current ratios suggest, because agencies expect the sponsor to keep leverage high through dividend recaps and debt-funded acquisitions. Weak liquidity or a looming maturity can cap a rating on its own.",
    example: "A rough, illustrative guide for a company of middling business risk: about [[2–3x]] debt/EBITDA fits BBB, [[3–4x]] BB and [[5x]] or more single B. A market leader in a stable industry might hold BBB at [[3.5x]]; a small cyclical company might sit around BB− to B+ at the same leverage. Each agency's grid differs by industry.",
    trap: "Don't quote the company's own adjusted EBITDA. Agencies build their own, usually lower, so their leverage figure can be a turn (1.0x) or more above what the company reports.",
