    classic: true,
    q: "How does a lender's analysis of a company differ from an equity investor's?",
    a: "A lender's upside is capped at interest and principal, so it focuses on the downside: can the company pay interest and repay or refinance, and what's recovered if it can't? That means stable free cash flow, leverage and coverage, liquidity, maturities, collateral and covenants. An equity investor owns the upside, so growth and exit value matter far more.",
    why: "The payoffs are asymmetric. If the company doubles in value, the lender still gets par; if it fails, the lender can lose most of its money. So credit work stress-tests bad cases instead of leaning on the base case: how far can EBITDA fall before coverage drops below 1.0x or a covenant trips, and how much enterprise value sits below the debt? That cushion is often measured as loan-to-value: debt ÷ enterprise value. Lenders also judge the sponsor's and management's behavior, since dividends and debt-funded acquisitions move value away from them.",
    example: "Enterprise value [[$1,000M]], debt [[$600M]]: loan-to-value is 60%, so $400M of equity absorbs losses first. Value can fall 40% before the lenders' principal is at risk. A buyer counting on growth cares about the upside; the lender cares about the $400M cushion.",
    trap: "Good equity stories can be bad credits: a fast-growing company burning cash may be a fine equity bet but can't service much debt. And a dull, steady business can be an excellent credit."
