    classic: true,
    q: "What's the difference between secured and unsecured debt, and between senior and subordinated debt?",
    a: "Security is about collateral: secured debt has a lien on specific assets and is repaid first from their value. Seniority is about contractual ranking: senior debt is paid before subordinated debt from whatever is left. They're separate ideas, so senior unsecured notes rank ahead of subordinated notes but behind secured debt, up to the value of the collateral.",
    why: "In default, value flows down a ladder. Secured creditors take their collateral's value first, first lien before second lien. The value left over goes to general unsecured creditors, such as senior notes, trade payables and any shortfall on secured claims, who share pro rata. Subordinated debt has agreed to be paid only after senior debt is paid in full, and equity gets what remains. Second-lien debt is lien-subordinated only: it ranks behind the first lien on the shared collateral, but any shortfall is a senior unsecured claim, equal with the notes. The lower the rank, the higher the yield investors demand.",
    trap: "Senior doesn't mean secured. The follow-up: can senior notes end up behind trade payables? Not by contract, since they rank equally, but structurally they can, if the payables sit in subsidiaries that don't guarantee the notes.",
