    why: "Modified duration is the percentage price change for a 1-point change in yield: the slope of the price–yield curve at today's yield. The curve bends, so the slope misses more the bigger the move; for small moves duration is enough, for large ones add a convexity term. Duration is also a measure of time: Macaulay duration is the present-value-weighted average time to the cash flows, 7.66 years here, and modified duration divides it by one plus the yield per period. Traders often quote DV01, the dollar change for a 1 bp move.",
