    q: "A 5-year bond with a 6% coupon trades at 95. What are its current yield and yield to maturity, and why do they differ?",
    a: "Current yield is 6.32%: the $6 annual coupon ÷ the $95 price. Yield to maturity (YTM) is about 7.21%, because it also counts the $5 gain as the price pulls to par by maturity, and the timing of every payment. YTM is the discount rate that sets the present value of all the cash flows equal to the price.",
    why: "Current yield measures income only, so it understates the return on a discount bond, whose price rises to par, and overstates it on a premium bond, whose price drifts down to par. YTM is the bond's internal rate of return, and it assumes you hold to maturity, every payment arrives on time and coupons are reinvested at the same yield. Sell early and your return depends on the price you get then. YTM is the standard way to compare bonds with different coupons, prices and maturities; current yield is a quick read on cash income, which matters to investors who live off coupons.",
    formula: "Current yield = annual coupon ÷ price\nPrice = sum of each cash flow ÷ (1 + YTM ÷ 2)^(half-years until paid)\nShortcut: YTM ≈ (coupon + (100 − price) ÷ years) ÷ ((100 + price) ÷ 2)",
    example: "Coupon [[$6]] a year, paid [[twice a year]], price [[95]], [[5]] years to maturity. Current yield: 6 ÷ 95 = 6.32%. Shortcut YTM: (6 + 5 ÷ 5) ÷ 97.5 = 7.18%. Solved exactly, YTM is 7.21%.",
    trap: "The follow-up: what about a premium bond? The order flips to coupon rate above current yield above YTM, since the price falls toward par. At a discount, as here, it's 6.00% < 6.32% < 7.21%; at par all three are equal."
