    q: "A company's 5-year bond yields 8.0% and the 5-year Treasury yields 4.0%. What's the spread, and what is it paying you for?",
    a: "400 bps. The spread is the extra yield over a Treasury of matching maturity, and it pays for credit risk: the losses you expect from default, plus a premium for bearing that uncertainty and for owning a bond that's harder to sell. A bond's yield can rise because Treasury yields rise or because its spread widens.",
    why: "Expected loss is roughly the annual default probability times the loss given default, which is one minus the recovery rate. It's usually well below the spread; the rest compensates investors for the chance that defaults cluster in a recession, when losses hurt most, and for weaker liquidity. That's why spreads widen sharply in downturns: expected defaults rise and investors' appetite for risk falls at the same time. Spreads also let you compare credits free of the rate level, so bonds issued years apart can be compared even though their yields differ.",
