    module: "ib-credit",
    topic: "Repricing a term loan",
    level: 3,
    type: "qa",
    q: "A $1.0B term loan pays SOFR plus 400 bps, and similar loans now price at SOFR plus 325. Its 101 soft call expires in two months. Should the company reprice now or wait?",
    a: "Wait. Repricing saves 75 bps, $7.5M a year or about $0.6M a month. Doing it now triggers the 101 soft call, a $10.0M premium, to capture only two extra months of savings, about $1.25M. Line up the repricing to close just after the soft call expires, unless the market looks likely to turn.",
    why: "A repricing is an amendment that cuts the margin without replacing the loan. Loan investors can't block it: the credit agreement lets the borrower replace lenders who don't consent by having their loans bought out at par, so each lender either accepts the lower spread or is taken out. The soft call is the lenders' main protection, and it applies only to transactions meant to lower the loan's yield, so a refinancing for an acquisition, for example, usually escapes it. That's why repricing waves follow spread tightening, and why loan prices rarely rise much above par.",
    example: "[[$1.0B]] × ([[4.00%]] − [[3.25%]]) = $7.5M a year, or $0.625M a month. Repricing now captures $1.25M more savings but pays a [[1%]] premium of $10.0M: $8.75M worse than waiting [[two]] months.",
    trap: "Don't forget the other costs: arrangers charge a fee for a repricing, and lenders who refuse must be replaced. The saving isn't guaranteed either: if spreads widen before launch, the window can close."
