    q: "A $200M revolver has a springing 7.0x first-lien leverage covenant, tested only when more than 35% of it is drawn. How does that work, and why would the banks accept it?",
    a: "The test applies only at quarter-ends when more than $70M is drawn; otherwise there's no financial test. Banks accept it because their risk is small while the line is barely used. Companies lean on their revolvers when they're struggling, so the covenant springs into force when it matters, letting the banks stop funding and renegotiate.",
    why: "Springing covenants are the compromise in covenant-lite deals: term loan investors give up maintenance tests, while the revolver banks keep a light one of their own. The level is usually set loosely, well above expected leverage, so it catches real distress rather than a weak quarter. Letters of credit are often excluded from the drawn amount. And because it's tested only at quarter-ends, a company can repay drawings just before the test date to stay under the trigger, though that uses cash exactly when it's scarce.",
