    q: "An indenture allows new debt if pro forma fixed-charge coverage stays at 2.0x or more, plus a $100M general basket. EBITDA is $200M and fixed charges $80M. How much new 8% debt can the company raise?",
    a: "$350M. The ratio test allows fixed charges up to $200M ÷ 2.0 = $100M, so $20M more interest, which supports $250M at 8%. The general basket adds $100M that doesn't have to meet the ratio. Order matters: use the ratio first, because basket debt incurred first counts in the ratio test and would cut ratio capacity to $150M.",
    why: "Incurrence covenants work as a rule plus exceptions. The rule is the ratio test, and debt raised under it is called ratio debt. The exceptions, called baskets, permit set amounts regardless of the ratio: a general basket, a credit facilities basket and baskets for items such as capital leases and acquired debt. Many baskets grow with the company, sized as the greater of a dollar amount and a percentage of EBITDA. Restricted payments work the same way, with a builder basket that grows with net income. Added up, a package can allow far more debt than its headline ratio suggests, so investors read the baskets closely.",
    formula: "Maximum fixed charges = EBITDA ÷ minimum coverage\nRatio debt = (maximum − current fixed charges) ÷ interest rate\nTotal capacity = ratio debt + available baskets",
